<Docs title="DS Bridge Figma plugin" description="What the DS Bridge Figma plugin does (tokens both ways, what is missing in Figma, Compare with code) and how to install it in Figma desktop." sections={sections}>
  <section aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What it does</h2>
    <p>Your tokens sync both ways. DS Bridge tells you what is missing in your Figma and checks that it has caught up with the code. The repository stays the source of truth. It works on the free Figma plan, and nothing is written to main directly.</p>

    <h3>Pull: tokens from code into Figma</h3>
    <p>Pull brings the latest tokens from the library into Figma Variables, one collection per tier with its modes, showing the plan before anything is written. Your own Figma edits are kept, and tokens changed on both sides show as conflicts for you to decide.</p>
    <p>Pull also says which components from the code your Figma file does not have yet. Your own components count: they are matched by name, so "Forms/Select" is the code's Select. Components from libraries are not counted yet.</p>

    <h3>Push: your variable edits back for review</h3>
    <p>Push lists the variables you changed, with the contrast they move in every brand and theme. Blocking problems, such as a pair that fails contrast, disable the send.</p>
    <p>Your edits go to <strong>Send for review</strong> or to <strong>Try in an exploration</strong>. Depending on your repository, they become a pull request with a change table, contrast before and after, and the risk tier, or a suggestion for your design system lead. A pull request that only changes token values can ship once your checks pass; renamed or removed tokens wait for a developer.</p>

    <h3>Compare with code</h3>
    <p><strong>Compare with code</strong>, in the Pull tab, compares the component sets Bridge drew in your file with images of them rendered from code, and says <strong>Matches the code</strong> or <strong>Differs from the code</strong>. Your own components, matched by name, say that Bridge can't compare them yet. Nothing in the file changes.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="install">
    <h2 id="install">Install it</h2>
    <p>The Figma Community listing is not live yet: it has been in review since 2026-10-16. Until then, install it in the Figma desktop app:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><Link href={PLUGIN_ZIP_URL}>Download the plugin</Link> and unzip it. The link always gives the latest version.</li>
      <li>In Figma, open <strong>Plugins &gt; Development &gt; Import plugin from manifest...</strong> and pick <code>manifest.json</code> in the <code>bridge-figma-plugin</code> folder.</li>
      <li>Run it from <strong>Plugins &gt; Development &gt; DS Bridge</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <h3>Update it</h3>
    <p>Download the zip again and unzip it over the old <code>bridge-figma-plugin</code> folder, replacing its files. Nothing to import again: Figma runs the new version and keeps your sign-in.</p>
    <p>If you imported a second copy, Figma lists DS Bridge twice and you can't tell which one runs. Open <strong>Plugins &gt; Development &gt; Manage plugins in development</strong> and choose <strong>Remove local version</strong> on the older one.</p>
    <p>Developers can build it from <Link href={GITHUB_URL}>the repository</Link> instead: <code>npm install</code>, then <code>npm run plugin:build</code>, and import <code>apps/figma-plugin/manifest.json</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="need">
    <h2 id="need">What you need</h2>
    <p>A Bridge account in your team, the one you use in Studio. In the plugin, choose <strong>Sign in with Bridge</strong>: your browser opens Studio, you pick your team, and the plugin connects to your design system. No token to paste. When the sign-in expires, the plugin asks you to sign in again.</p>
    <p>The sign-in stays on this computer, never in the Figma file. GitHub only, no GitLab or Bitbucket.</p>
    <p>Developers can connect with their own fine-grained GitHub token instead, under <strong>For developers</strong> in the plugin's Settings.</p>
  </section>
</Docs>
